 "overview": {
  "summary": "The frequency source of the Wideband AC Voltage option, and the only board in the instrument that exists solely to make a variable-frequency square wave and filter it into a sine. A6 generates 1.1 MHz to 30 MHz with two-digit resolution at a nominal full-scale output of 700 mV rms, and hands it to the Wideband Output assembly (A5) through connector J1 and a 75 ohm coaxial cable. Four blocks make it up. A phase-locked loop and divider chain -- comparator U15A making an 8 MHz TTL reference from the instrument's CLK/CLK* sine, synthesiser U1 (MC145145) with its two divide-by-N counters and phase detector, loop amplifier U2, varactors CR1/CR2, vco U3 (MC1648) running 32 to 64 MHz, flip-flop U4 and binary counter U5 dividing it down, and multiplexer U6 picking one of five octaves -- produces the ECL-level square wave. An amplitude control amplifier built round transistor array U9 (CA1302) sets its level from the WB AMPLITUDE CONTROL dc that A5's thermal rms sensor loop sends back. An X10 wideband amplifier, Q4/Q5/Q6, raises it to 1.4 to 6.0 V p-p. And five five-pole LC filters, one per octave (1-2, 2-4, 4-8, 8-16 and 16-32 MHz), each switched in by FETs and PIN diodes under transistor arrays U12 and U13, turn the square wave into a sine before output driver Q7. The board makes none of its own power: +5 LH, -5 LH, +17 S and -17 S all arrive from the Regulator/Guard Crossing assembly (A17) on P101.",
  "sections": "§2-215, §2-216 … §2-221",
  "option": "THIS BOARD IS ONLY FITTED TO INSTRUMENTS WITH THE -03 WIDEBAND AC VOLTAGE OPTION. On a 5700A or 5720A without option -03 there is no A6, no A5, no Type N connector on the front panel, and the two card slots in front of A7 are empty — nothing is missing or has been removed. Figure 7-6, Table 6-7 and §5-5 are all captioned \"(Option -03)\" for the same reason. A6 and A5 are always fitted together: the module is the pair, A6 the oscillator and A5 the output amplifier, and neither is useful alone. If the instrument in front of you has a Type N WIDEBAND connector on the front panel then it has the option and both boards are in it; A6 sits between A5 and A7 in the analog card cage (Figure 4-5), with A5 nearer the front panel.",
  "note": "A6 makes no supply that Chapter 2 tabulates. §2-217 lists what it does make from the four rails that arrive on P101: chokes L1/L2/L3/L4 filter -5 LH, +5 LH, -17 S and +17 S on entry; L12 and C49 buffer -5 LH again into -5F; R66 and R67 divide +5 LH to make the +2.5 V reference for the comparators in U7, U10 and U11; VR1 with R39 drops +17 S to +12; and VR5, CR8, CR9, R40 and R41 with VR4 make -12, -11 and -9.5 from -17 S. Two commons come in and they are not the same net: LH COM on P101 18A/18C, which C1 and C3 return to, and S COM on 24A/24C, which is SCOM and what both test point loops are on."
 },
